From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 01:40:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] EXPERIMENT: misc: h713: preload the HDMI-RX EDID and release HPD

With 0087 the THDMIRX window is reachable, so the next question is whether a
source will see this board as a display at all. It currently does not: the host
GPU on the other end of the cable reports its HDMI connector "disconnected",
because nothing asserts HPD and there is no EDID to read.

Load one and let go of HPD.

The EDID is generated, not copied. Borrowing a monitor's EDID off the host
would have been quicker and would have made the projector announce someone
else's model string and physical size, which is a lie that survives into every
later capture experiment. This one advertises 1920x1080p60 (148.5 MHz, the
CEA VIC 16 timing) as the preferred mode, with a 160x90 cm image and the
product name "H713 HDMI-RX". edid-decode parses it with no warnings and no
conformity failures.

The write path is the Synopsys EDID RAM port, per the peer RE port: set
WRITE_EN with slave address 0x50 in DMA_CONFIG11, stream bytes through the
auto-incrementing DMA_CONFIG10, then drop both enables so source-side DDC reads
reach the RAM. HPD is held low across the reload through SCDC_CONFIG.HPDLOW and
released afterwards, with a sustained low rather than a flicker -- the peer
measured that a brief pulse does not invalidate a source's "no display" state.

Two deliberate omissions, both so a negative result stays interpretable:

  - No readback check. The peer records that this RAM reads back as zero even
    on runs where a source read the data correctly, so a readback assertion
    here would manufacture a false failure. The honest verification is what the
    source sees over DDC, which is measured from the host.

  - No vendor controller-enable sequence. It writes the wrapper at 0x06840000,
    whose power state has never been checked, and an access to an unpowered
    block on this SoC hangs the bus with no abort. Leaving it out turns this
    into a real experiment: if the source reads the EDID anyway, DDC does not
    need the controller init, and if it does not, that is the next thing to add
    -- against a known window rather than a guess.

Note that HPD is driven here through SCDC_CONFIG inside THDMIRX, NOT through
0x07091014. That register is the peer's HPD-pin control, it is in a block no DT
node describes, and reading it is what hard-locked the board earlier. If
SCDC_CONFIG is sufficient, it never needs to be touched.

The DT window grows to 0x10000 because the EDID RAM port lives at +0x4424,
outside the 0x1000 that 0087 mapped.

RESULT: the EDID write does nothing, and the reason is worth the boot.

DMA_CONFIG11 will not take a write. The driver sets WRITE_EN with slave 0x50
and then clears only the enables, so the slave address should stay parked at
0x50; it reads 0x00000000 before and after. Confirmed directly from userspace,
which is possible now because this driver holds the TV domains up:

  SCDC_CONFIG    0x050c0300  <- 0xFFFF1002  reads back 0xFFFF1002   writable
  DMA_CONFIG11   0x050c4428  <- 0x000000D0  reads back 0x00000000   dead
  GLOBAL_SWENABLE 0x050c0024              0x00000000               not enabled

So HPD control is live and the EDID port is not. Three things were then ruled
out, each cheaply:

  - Global enable. Writing the peer's full SWENABLE value 0x3B01 sticks
    (reads back 0x00003B01) and DMA_CONFIG11 still does not.
  - Reset. RST_BUS_TVCAP is CCU 0xd88 BIT(16), and 0xd88 already reads
    0x00010001 -- the block was never held in reset.
  - The bank being merely quiet. 0x050c0000..0x3c is full of structured values
    (0x70F80029, 0xFE000115, 0x03FF00FF, ...) while 0x050c4400..0x443c is
    uniformly zero. Two banks in one window, one answering and one not.

The live hypothesis is the H713 wrapper the peer documents at 0x06800800
(port-select, PHY reset) and 0x06840000 (per-port state), which their
controller_enable writes FIRST, before any Synopsys register. If the EDID RAM
is per-port and no port is selected, a dead bank reading zero is exactly what
that looks like.

That window is NOT probed here on purpose. No DT node describes it, its power
state is unverified, and an access to an unpowered block on this SoC hard-locks
the board -- which has already cost one power cycle this session. It gets a
domain check first, not a speculative read.

OUT OF SERIES. Applies after 0087.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c b/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c
--- a/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c
@@ -1,18 +1,22 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
 /*
- * H713 TVFE/TVCAP bring-up probe.
+ * H713 TVFE/TVCAP bring-up probe, and an HDMI-RX EDID preload.
  *
  * Minimal, deliberately: attach the two TV power domains, enable the TV
- * clocks, read a few words of the HDMI-RX window, print them, and stop. No
+ * clocks, then load an EDID into the HDMI-RX EDID RAM and release HPD. No
  * interrupt is claimed and no GPIO is touched, because the vendor tvtop node
  * claims GIC_SPI 110 (owned by the KMS display driver) and carries PB5, the
  * backlight and fan enable.
  *
- * Existing to answer one question: whether the HDMI-RX register windows become
- * readable once TVFE and TVCAP are powered. Until they are, a read of that
- * space hangs the bus with no abort and no watchdog.
+ * What is deliberately NOT done here is the vendor controller-enable sequence.
+ * It writes the wrapper at 0x06840000, whose power state has never been
+ * checked, and an access to an unpowered block on this SoC hangs the bus with
+ * no abort and no watchdog. Skipping it makes this a real experiment: if the
+ * source reads the EDID anyway, the controller init is not needed for DDC, and
+ * if it does not, that is the next thing to add.
  */
 #include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
@@ -20,6 +24,42 @@
 #include <linux/pm_domain.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 
+/* Synopsys DW-HDMI-RX offsets within the THDMIRX window at 0x050c0000. */
+#define THDMIRX_SCDC_CONFIG		0x0300
+#define  THDMIRX_HPDLOW			BIT(1)
+#define THDMIRX_DMA_CONFIG10		0x4424	/* EDID RAM write port */
+#define THDMIRX_DMA_CONFIG11		0x4428	/* EDID RAM access control */
+#define  THDMIRX_EDID_READ_EN		BIT(8)
+#define  THDMIRX_EDID_WRITE_EN		BIT(7)
+#define  THDMIRX_EDID_SLAVE_ADDR_MASK	GENMASK(6, 0)
+
+#define H713_EDID_DDC_ADDR		0x50
+
+/*
+ * A generated EDID 1.4 advertising 1920x1080p60 (148.5 MHz, VIC 16) as the
+ * preferred timing. Generated rather than copied off a monitor: a borrowed
+ * EDID would make the projector announce someone else's model string and
+ * physical size. Verified with edid-decode -- no warnings, checksum 0xC9.
+ */
+static const u8 h713_hdmi_rx_edid[128] = {
+	0x00, 0xff, 0xff, 0xff, 0xff, 0xff, 0xff, 0x00,
+	0x23, 0x30, 0x13, 0x07, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
+	0x01, 0x24, 0x01, 0x04, 0xa2, 0xa0, 0x5a, 0x78,
+	0x02, 0xee, 0x91, 0xa3, 0x54, 0x4c, 0x99, 0x26,
+	0x0f, 0x50, 0x54, 0x20, 0x00, 0x00, 0x01, 0x01,
+	0x01, 0x01, 0x01, 0x01, 0x01, 0x01, 0x01, 0x01,
+	0x01, 0x01, 0x01, 0x01, 0x02, 0x3a, 0x80, 0x18,
+	0x71, 0x38, 0x2d, 0x40, 0x58, 0x2c, 0x45, 0x00,
+	0x40, 0x84, 0x63, 0x00, 0x00, 0x1e, 0x00, 0x00,
+	0x00, 0xfd, 0x00, 0x17, 0x3d, 0x0f, 0x46, 0x11,
+	0x00, 0x0a, 0x20, 0x20, 0x20, 0x20, 0x20, 0x20,
+	0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0xfc, 0x00, 0x48, 0x37, 0x31,
+	0x33, 0x20, 0x48, 0x44, 0x4d, 0x49, 0x2d, 0x52,
+	0x58, 0x0a, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x10, 0x00, 0x20,
+	0x20, 0x20, 0x20, 0x20, 0x20, 0x20, 0x20, 0x20,
+	0x20, 0x20, 0x20, 0x20, 0x00, 0xc9,
+};
+
 struct h713_tvcap {
 	struct device	*dev;
 	struct device	*pd_tvfe;
@@ -29,6 +69,49 @@
 	void __iomem	*thdmirx;
 };
 
+static void h713_rx_update(struct h713_tvcap *tv, u32 off, u32 mask, u32 val)
+{
+	u32 reg = readl(tv->thdmirx + off);
+
+	reg = (reg & ~mask) | (val & mask);
+	writel(reg, tv->thdmirx + off);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Load the EDID RAM. Readback is not attempted: the peer RE port records that
+ * this RAM reads back as zero even on runs where a source read the data
+ * correctly, so a readback check here would manufacture a false failure. The
+ * only honest verification is what the source sees over DDC.
+ */
+static void h713_rx_write_edid(struct h713_tvcap *tv, const u8 *edid, size_t len)
+{
+	size_t i;
+
+	/* Hold HPD low across the reload so the source re-reads afterwards. */
+	h713_rx_update(tv, THDMIRX_SCDC_CONFIG, THDMIRX_HPDLOW, THDMIRX_HPDLOW);
+
+	h713_rx_update(tv, THDMIRX_DMA_CONFIG11,
+		       THDMIRX_EDID_READ_EN | THDMIRX_EDID_WRITE_EN |
+			       THDMIRX_EDID_SLAVE_ADDR_MASK,
+		       THDMIRX_EDID_WRITE_EN | H713_EDID_DDC_ADDR);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < len; i++)
+		writel(edid[i], tv->thdmirx + THDMIRX_DMA_CONFIG10);
+
+	/* Drop both enables so source-side DDC reads reach the RAM. */
+	h713_rx_update(tv, THDMIRX_DMA_CONFIG11,
+		       THDMIRX_EDID_READ_EN | THDMIRX_EDID_WRITE_EN, 0);
+
+	/*
+	 * A sustained low is what invalidates the source's "nothing there"
+	 * state; a brief flicker does not. The peer measured this.
+	 */
+	msleep(500);
+	h713_rx_update(tv, THDMIRX_SCDC_CONFIG, THDMIRX_HPDLOW, 0);
+
+	dev_info(tv->dev, "wrote %zu EDID bytes, released HPD\n", len);
+}
+
 static int h713_tvcap_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
@@ -75,23 +158,26 @@
 
 	dev_info(dev, "TVFE+TVCAP powered, %d clocks enabled\n", tv->num_clks);
 
-	/*
-	 * The moment of truth. If the domains were the whole story this
-	 * returns data; if it hangs, the board stops here and the next boot
-	 * knows the domains are not sufficient.
-	 */
 	tv->thdmirx = devm_platform_ioremap_resource(pdev, 0);
 	if (IS_ERR(tv->thdmirx)) {
 		ret = PTR_ERR(tv->thdmirx);
 		goto err_disable_clks;
 	}
 
-	dev_info(dev, "reading THDMIRX -- if this is the last line, it hung\n");
-	for (i = 0; i < 8; i++)
+	for (i = 0; i < 4; i++)
 		dev_info(dev, "  thdmirx+0x%02x = 0x%08x\n",
 			 i * 4, readl(tv->thdmirx + i * 4));
 
-	dev_info(dev, "THDMIRX window is readable\n");
+	dev_info(dev, "scdc_config = 0x%08x, dma_config11 = 0x%08x\n",
+		 readl(tv->thdmirx + THDMIRX_SCDC_CONFIG),
+		 readl(tv->thdmirx + THDMIRX_DMA_CONFIG11));
+
+	h713_rx_write_edid(tv, h713_hdmi_rx_edid, sizeof(h713_hdmi_rx_edid));
+
+	dev_info(dev, "scdc_config = 0x%08x, dma_config11 = 0x%08x (after)\n",
+		 readl(tv->thdmirx + THDMIRX_SCDC_CONFIG),
+		 readl(tv->thdmirx + THDMIRX_DMA_CONFIG11));
+
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, tv);
 	return 0;
 
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -1582,7 +1582,8 @@
 		 */
 		hdmirx_probe: hdmirx-probe@50c0000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,h713-tvcap-probe";
-			reg = <0x050c0000 0x1000>;
+			/* 0x10000: the EDID RAM port is at +0x4424, past 0x1000. */
+			reg = <0x050c0000 0x10000>;
 			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_TVCAP>,
 				 <&ccu CLK_BUS_CAP_300M>,
 				 <&ccu CLK_VINCAP_DMA>,
